SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DRC: One year on, 'State of Siege' used as a tool to crush dissent","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-05-10","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/05/drc-one-year-on-state-of-siege/","claim":"Amnesty reported that the state of siege in North Kivu and Ituri was being used to crush dissent, with two activists killed by security forces and dozens of critics arbitrarily detained on trumped-up charges.","locator":"paragraph beginning 'A State of Siege...' / lines 64-67","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous NGO briefing with named cases, interviews, and direct discussion of the emergency regime.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 target period; it describes a measure imposed in 2021 and still active in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Tshisekedi directly declared and extended the state of siege; the specific abuses are security-force conduct under a regime he kept in place and tolerated after notice.","contrary_evidence":["The government said the state of siege aimed to protect civilians from armed groups.","Armed-group violence also continued in the affected provinces."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DRC: Stop using prolonged state of siege as excuse to crush protests","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-09-27","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/09/drc-stop-using-prolonged-state-of-siege-as-excuse-to-crush-protests/","claim":"Amnesty said that demanding accountability from DRC authorities in the two eastern provinces had become particularly risky under the prolonged state of siege, with dozens of critics arbitrarily detained and sometimes prosecuted by military courts, while authorities threatened to crush new protests.","locator":"lines 65-67","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous advocacy report focused on protests, detention, and emergency-rule abuse, with direct quotations from Amnesty.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022, after more than a year of the state of siege.","ruler_attribution":"Attribution is strong for the emergency regime because Tshisekedi kept the state of siege in force; the arrests are carried out by military authorities operating under that regime.","contrary_evidence":["The protests were also directed at MONUSCO and the M23 crisis, so not all security pressure originated from the presidency.","The government framed the measure as necessary for security."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2022: Democratic Republic of Congo","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2021-12","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2022/country-chapters/democratic-republic-congo","claim":"HRW reported that in April 2022 police killed five people and wounded at least eight others during peaceful protests in Goma, Butembo, and Beni territory, and that security forces and violent mobs later killed at least 10 people in Nyiragongo territory.","locator":"lines 78-78","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Major rights-monitor synthesis with concrete casualty figures and a clear description of the protest crackdown.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The incidents occurred in 2022 and are squarely within the target period.","ruler_attribution":"Attribution is partial but meaningful: the killings were by police and security forces under a president who controlled the national security chain and maintained the emergency environment.","contrary_evidence":["Some violence was also carried out by violent mobs and not solely by state forces.","The protests were connected to local security tensions, not only presidential policy."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.6","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2022: Democratic Republic of Congo","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2021-12","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2022/country-chapters/democratic-republic-congo","claim":"HRW reported that Tshisekedi later apologized to children involved in a police round-up, but only after he appointed the police commander responsible for the round-up, François Kabeya, as mayor of Goma.","locator":"lines 79-79","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Specific attribution to a presidential appointment and a contemporaneous response to abuse, captured in a major rights report.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 target period; the appointment and apology are both discussed in the 2022 report.","ruler_attribution":"Direct for the appointment, which weakens the remedial value of the apology and suggests tolerance of abusive policing rather than accountability.","contrary_evidence":["The president did apologize to the affected children.","The underlying police action was carried out by local security personnel, not by the president personally."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Three journalists tortured by DRC intelligence agency","publisher":"Reporters Without Borders","publication_date":"2022-05-23","url":"https://rsf.org/en/three-journalists-tortured-drc-intelligence-agency","claim":"RSF reported that the National Intelligence Agency detained and tortured three journalists in Boende on 18 May 2022 and said that at least ten journalists had been arrested or threatened by the agency since the start of the year.","locator":"lines 327-337","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed contemporaneous press-freedom report with named victims, dates, and the agency involved.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The ANR is a state security agency; the evidence supports a strong state nexus and tolerated abuse, though it does not show a personal presidential order for the torture.","contrary_evidence":["RSF notes the abuse by the local ANR director rather than a directly documented presidential instruction.","The government could still have ordered an investigation, but the report says the abuse reflected close control and intimidation."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.8","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Journalists targeted by fighters in eastern DRC’s Nord-Kivu province","publisher":"Reporters Without Borders","publication_date":"2022-06-24","url":"https://rsf.org/en/journalists-targeted-fighters-eastern-drc-s-nord-kivu-province","claim":"RSF said journalists in North Kivu were facing cyber-harassment and frequent death threats from Congolese government supporters, and urged the government to protect journalists and prosecute attackers.","locator":"lines 330-340","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous regional press-freedom monitor with named journalists and a direct warning to the government.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but relevant: the threats came from government supporters and reflect a tolerated intimidation climate under the ruling network.","contrary_evidence":["Some threats also came from M23-side actors, so the environment was not exclusively state-driven.","This source does not prove a direct presidential order."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.8","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Freelance reporter abducted, held incommunicado in DRC","publisher":"Reporters Without Borders","publication_date":"2022-10-26","url":"https://rsf.org/en/freelance-reporter-abducted-held-incommunicado-drc","claim":"RSF reported that freelance reporter Steve Wembi was taken in an abduction-style arrest on 24 October 2022 and remained unlocated, while the government spokesman denied that the ANR was holding him.","locator":"lines 311-327","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Specific incident report with a named journalist, date of arrest, and a contemporaneous government denial.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Within the 2022 target period.","ruler_attribution":"Strong state nexus through the suspected intelligence-service involvement and the spokesman's response, though direct presidential direction is not shown.","contrary_evidence":["The government spokesman denied that the ANR held the journalist.","The source does not establish who physically seized him, only that he disappeared after the arrest."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.8","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNHCR gravely concerned by death toll of displaced in DR Congo's east","publisher":"UNHCR","publication_date":"2022-07-22","url":"https://www.unhcr.org/news/briefing-notes/unhcr-gravely-concerned-death-toll-displaced-dr-congos-east","claim":"UNHCR said simultaneous attacks in Ituri left 11 people dead and 250 homes looted and burned, that over 800 deaths from attacks had been recorded in Ituri since February, and that fighting between the Congolese army and M23 displaced more than 160,000 people and created power vacuums in Ituri and North Kivu.","locator":"lines 397-405","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"UN humanitarian briefing with concrete casualty and displacement figures and a direct note on government-troop redeployment effects.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly indirect: armed groups were the immediate perpetrators, but the report ties worsening civilian risk to the government's inability to hold territory and the security vacuum created by troop redeployment.","contrary_evidence":["The immediate attackers were armed groups, not the president personally.","The report notes conflict complexity and multiple militia actors."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.6","3B.7","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"RDC : 142 personnes tuées et près de 30 000 autres déplacées à la suite des combats dans l'Ouest (HCR)","publisher":"Radio Okapi","publication_date":"2022-10-12","url":"https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/10/12/actualite/securite/rdc-142-personnes-tuees-et-pres-de-30-000-autres-deplacees-la-suite","claim":"Radio Okapi reported UNHCR figures that more than 142 people had been killed in the Kwamouth intercommunal violence, about 27,000 people had been displaced, and the Congolese army had been deployed to restore order while the security situation remained tense.","locator":"lines 141-156","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous reporting of a UNHCR briefing with precise figures and the government's security response.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Mixed: the violence was intercommunal, but the president was responsible for the state response, including the decision to deploy the army; the report shows response after escalation rather than prevention.","contrary_evidence":["The clashes were rooted in local Teke-Yaka disputes over land and customary taxes.","Provincial authorities also set up their own crisis committee and multisector plan."],"lenses":["3B.6","3B.7","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Felix Tshisekedi ordonne la documentation des faits infractionnels commis dans le Grand Bandundu","publisher":"Radio Okapi","publication_date":"2022-10-09","url":"https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/10/09/actualite/politique/felix-tshisekedi-ordonne-la-documentation-des-faits-infractionnels","claim":"Radio Okapi reported that Tshisekedi ordered specialized government services to document the offences committed in the Kwamouth conflict in order to speed the restoration of public order in the Grand Bandundu.","locator":"lines 141-145","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct report of a presidential council-of-ministers instruction with a clear accountability and order-restoration purpose.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct presidential action: this is a ruler-issued instruction to investigate/document violence, though the report does not show completion or results.","contrary_evidence":["The report does not show that the documentation led to prosecutions or protection improvements.","The underlying conflict remained active despite the order."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"RDC : le bilan des manifestations anti-MONUSCO s’alourdit, 15 morts","publisher":"Radio Okapi","publication_date":"2022-07-27","url":"https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/07/27/actualite/politique/rdc-le-bilan-des-manifestations-anti-monusco-salourdit-15-morts","claim":"Radio Okapi reported that the two-day anti-MONUSCO protests had left 15 dead, including three UN peacekeepers and twelve civilians, and that the government said investigations had begun to determine who was responsible.","locator":"lines 150-152","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous local reporting of a major protest crackdown with explicit casualty totals and an official note that inquiries had started.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Mixed: the protests and deaths involved multiple actors, but the national government was in charge of the security response and publicly acknowledged the need for investigation.","contrary_evidence":["Some deaths were of UN peacekeepers rather than protesters.","The source does not identify a single chain of command for each death."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.9","3B.10"]}

Search ledger:
- Candidates found: 41
- Documents opened: 20
